y with with the ochre  V review and we submitted figures  actually showed that the BCR that's the  cost-benefit number significantly  underestimates the benefits for instance  of HS 2 alongside rebalance the economy  through wider northern powerhouse  investments and this is also in only a  week since we saw leaks the government's  going to revise the Green Book anyway  because it doesn't advantage the north  and the Midlands priorities enough and  so you've got Tony on your program  arguing that the green book and the DFT  appraisal process is too generous to hs2  when actually all of government policies  that the benefits of major projects like  that of hs2 or northern powerhouse rail  so the north are being systematically  undervalued so I think I think it's a  nice way name the same arguments he's  always made and he doesn't seem to have  listened to any of the evidence that we  and others have presented him with at  the same time they we're talking about  an exorbitant amount of money here and  the costs have spiraled even if there is  a debate about exactly how much hs2 is  going to cost and I guess what labaki  would argue is he's not saying that you  know in an ideal world would be great to  have a high-speed rail link plus  connections to cities and towns in the  north of England but you're saying is  this the best way to spend each eight  billion pounds so I mean we would argue  that it is and the reality for that is  that there is no other way to underpin  rebalance into British economy so you do  need connections for instance from  cities like Birmingham to leads and more  capacity to get down to London if you're  going to continue to rebalance the  economy and what the damage is that  there isn't an alternative that adds up  so he's talked in great detail on your  program about these conventional  upgrades well there is no plan for that  that stacks up and actually when you do  try and upgrade the West Coast Main Line  it still costs billions and billio